        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/3f5db4d9-1463-4fa9-9114-78a10e2a7fc0,1775878985380/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C0%2C6240%2C3510%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='a mother holds a bloody notebook' width='620' height='349' title='Ola Rihan clutches the bloody notebook belonging to her nine-year-old daughter, Ritaj Rihan, who was shot and killed in her tent school in northern Gaza on Thursday, April 9, 2026. Rihan says her daughter&apos;s death is proof that there is no safety in any part of Gaza despite the six-month ceasefire.'/><p>Tent schools like the one where nine-year-old Ritaj Rihan was killed by gunfire this week are symbolic of the fragile hope Palestinians have for the future and the paralyzing fear they still live with today. Six months after Israel and Hamas reached the first phase of a ceasefire that was supposed to end fighting in Gaza, humanitarian organizations have given it a failing grade.</p>",
